<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Gold Coast Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1821 CE – 1957 CE · capital Accra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British colony on the coast of modern Ghana, which grew from a string of forts into the first sub-Saharan African colony to win independence, as Ghana in 1957.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1821 the British crown took over the forts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/company-of-merchants-trading-to-africa">Company of Merchants</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> and placed them under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">Charles MacCarthy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. He was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a> in 1824. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-maclean">George Maclean</a>, president of the merchant council from 1830, made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> in 1831 and extended British jurisdiction among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a>, formalised in the Bond of 1844.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> bought the Danish forts in 1850 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-gold-coast">Dutch Gold Coast</a> in 1872, and after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> in 1874 declared a crown colony, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> from 1877. The colony's economy rested on gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and above all cocoa, of which it became the largest exporter in the world. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordon-guggisberg">Gordon Guggisberg</a> built Takoradi harbour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achimota-school">Achimota School</a> in the 1920s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Riots in 1948 and the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-peoples-party">Convention People's Party</a> led to self-government. On 6 March 1957 the colony, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ashanti-colony">Ashanti Colony</a>, the Northern Territories and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-togoland">British Togoland</a>, became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gold-coast-colony.jpg" alt="Gold Coast Colony" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Gold_Coast_(1877%E2%80%931957).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1821 CE – 1957 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Accra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles MacCarthy, George Maclean, Gordon Guggisberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Akan, Ga, Ewe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gold Coast, British Gold Coast, Gold Coast (British colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1889&amp;focus=gold-coast-colony" aria-label="Gold Coast Colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4675 1970 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gold Coast Colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4995 2226L4955 2197M4995 2226L4972 2237M4995 2226L4998 2210M4995 2226L5025 2217M4995 2226L4965 2242M4995 2226L4997 651M4995 2226L4634 2117M4995 2226L4995 2226M4995 2226L4955 2183M4995 2226L4972 2079M4995 2226L5013 2186M4995 2226L5094 2192M4995 2226L4973 2212M4995 2226L4966 2239M4995 2226L4978 2224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4955 2197h0M4972 2237h0M4998 2210h0M5025 2217h0